The god class has one heavily-used method, `calculateInvoiceTotal`, buried among forty others:

```typescript
class BillingGodClass {
  calculateInvoiceTotal(invoice: Invoice): number {
    // 80 lines of untested logic, unchanged for now
  }
  // ...39 other methods
}
```

Step one: introduce an interface capturing just this method, and make the god class implement it — no behavior changes yet:

```typescript
interface InvoiceCalculator {
  calculateInvoiceTotal(invoice: Invoice): number;
}

class BillingGodClass implements InvoiceCalculator {
  calculateInvoiceTotal(invoice: Invoice): number {
    // identical body, still unchanged
  }
}
```

Callers can now depend on `InvoiceCalculator` instead of the god class directly. That single change creates a seam: a future extraction of the real logic into its own class can happen behind this interface, one caller at a time, without ever breaking one in the middle of the migration.
